title: Judiciary Reform
new-page: true
is-card: true
view-if: attorney_general_party = "R" and judiciary_timer <= 0
on-arrival: month_actions += 1; judiciary_timer += 6
card-image: img/portraits/Salmon-P-Chase.webp
tags: cotus_affairs, cabinet

= Judiciary Reform

The federal judiciary remains one of the greatest obstacles to Reconstruction. Many judges are holdovers from antebellum days, sympathetic to states’ rights and hostile to civil rights enforcement. <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radical**</span> legislation is regularly struck down or obstructed by conservative courts.

[? if judicial_reform <= 0 : Pressure is mounting to bring the courts in line with the goals of Reconstruction, but such a move would trigger fierce opposition from <span style="color: #9D2933;">**Moderates**</span> and <span style="color: #B5651D;">**Conservative**</span> allies. ?]
[? if judicial_reform == 1 : We have begun reshaping the courts, replacing some of the worst obstructionists. But many remain who would block land redistribution or civil rights enforcement. ?] 
[? if judicial_reform >= 2 : Our reforms have largely succeeded. The courts now lean toward upholding Reconstruction laws, and more transformative policies are within reach. ?]

- @reform: Reshape the judiciary in line with Reconstruction.
- @Act: Pass a new Judicial Circuits Act
- @minor_reforms: Pursue limited judicial reforms.
- @as_is: Avoid provoking the courts.
- @easy_discard
- @cancel_advisor_action

@reform
subtitle: -1 budget
unavailable-subtitle: Not enough budget.
choose-if: budget >= 1
on-arrival: judicial_reform += 1; budget -= 1; PoliticalCostPenalty += 1; radical_strength += 5 if radical_split = 0; conservative_dissent += 5; moderate_dissent += 5; freemen_rep += 5; freemen_rad += 3 if radical_split = 1;

<span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radical Republicans**</span> launch a full-scale campaign to overhaul the judiciary by impeaching obstructionist judges, appointing new justices, and passing legislation to expand the court's size. The move unleashes <span style="color: #B5651D;">**Conservative**</span> backlash but opens the door for ambitious reform.

[? if radical_split = 1 : This is precisely the kind of court-packing campaign the Radical Republicans have demanded since they walked out, and they are quick to remind freedmen who first called for it. ?]

@Act
subtitle: -2 budget
unavailable-subtitle: Not enough budget.
choose-if: budget >= 2
on-arrival: budget -= 2; PoliticalCostPenalty += 2; judicial_reform += 2; conservative_dissent += 5; moderate_dissent += 4; radical_strength += 3 if radical_split = 0; freemen_rep += 4; freemen_rad += 2 if radical_split = 1; middle_class_rep += 1;

Congress passes the Judicial Circuits Act, dramatically expanding federal court districts and enabling the appointment of dozens of loyal judges. <span style="color: #B5651D;">**Conservative**</span> factions decry it as court-packing, but the legislation survives constitutional challenge and shifts the judiciary firmly in favor of Reconstruction.

[? if radical_split = 1 : Radical Republican editors claim vindication, arguing the Act proves the regular party finally came around to their view, if only after they had already left it. ?]

@minor_reforms
on-arrival: judicial_reform += 0.5; moderate_dissent += 2; radical_strength += 2 if radical_split = 0; radical_dissent += 3 if radical_split = 0; judiciary_timer += 6

We target the most blatantly hostile judges for removal and quietly promote loyal legal minds. While cautious, this reform begins shifting the judiciary’s balance over time.

[? if radical_split = 1 : The Radical Republicans dismiss the effort as too little, too late, and use it to argue that only their party is serious about remaking the courts. ?]

@as_is
go-to: root